These assertions are written in line with the purpose of the Pex code generated by Microsoft's Pex framework. They are checking various properties of your test subject to ensure it behaves as expected. 

The tests start by setting up the prerequisite conditions (counts, indexes, whether the list contains the item etc.). Then, they make the change under test, i.e., calling the method or accessing the property of the subject. 

Finally, each test checks a property that should remain invariant after the test's application. This is done by looking at the result of the property access (or the new value of a variable), and verifying that it still holds some property we're interested in.

If the subject deviates from this property, it means that your test subject is not working correctly, and the test framework would report it as a failure. You may edit these assertions as needed for your context or needs.--------------------